    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L10-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Using Knowledge of Zeros",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Sketch a graph for a polynomial function \\(y=f(x)\\) that has 3 different zeros and \\(f(x)\\ge0\\) for all values of \\(x\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L10-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Using Knowledge of Zeros",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What is the smallest degree the polynomial could have?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L10-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Using Knowledge of Zeros",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What is a possible equation for the polynomial? Use graphing technology to see if your equation matches your sketch.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L10-ACTIVITY-027",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Using Knowledge of Zeros",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What is a possible equation of a polynomial function that has degree 5 but whose graph has exactly three horizontal intercepts and crosses the \\(x\\)-axis at all three intercepts? Explain why it is not possible to have a polynomial function…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L10-ACTIVITY-028",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Using Knowledge of Zeros",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Earlier, we learned to identify the zeros of a polynomial function from the factored expression. These factors let us figure out the points where the graph of the polynomial intersects the horizontal axis. The number of times a factor is…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L10-ACTIVITY-029",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Using Knowledge of Zeros",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For example, \\(y=(x+3)(x-1)(x-4)\\) has three factors with no duplicates. This results in a graph that looks a bit like a linear function near \\(x=\\text-3\\), \\(x=1\\), and \\(x=4\\) when we zoom in on each of those places.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L10-ACTIVITY-030",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Using Knowledge of Zeros",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(y=(x+3)(x-1)(x-4)\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L10-ACTIVITY-031",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Using Knowledge of Zeros",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(y=(x+3)(x-1)(x-4)\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L10-ACTIVITY-032",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Using Knowledge of Zeros",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We say that each factor, \\((x+3)\\), \\((x-1)\\), and \\((x-4)\\), has a multiplicity of 1.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L10-ACTIVITY-033",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Using Knowledge of Zeros",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For \\(y=(x+3)^2(x-4)\\), there are still three factors, but two of them are \\((x+3)\\). This results in a graph that looks a bit like a quadratic near \\(x=\\text-3\\) and a bit like a linear function near \\(x=4\\). We say that the factor…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L10-ACTIVITY-034",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Using Knowledge of Zeros",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Combining what we know about factors, degree, end behavior, the sign of the leading coefficient, and multiplicity gives us the ability to sketch polynomials written in factored form.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L10-ACTIVITY-035",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Using Knowledge of Zeros",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For example, consider what the graph of \\(y=(x+3)(x-4)^3\\) would look like. The factors help us identify that the function has zeros at -3 and 4. We also know that since \\((x+3)\\) has a multiplicity of 1 and \\((x-4)\\) has a multiplicity of…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L10-ACTIVITY-036",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Using Knowledge of Zeros",
      "SourceExcerpt": "How the outputs of a function change as we look at input values further and further from 0.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L10-ACTIVITY-037",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Using Knowledge of Zeros",
      "SourceExcerpt": "This function shows different end behavior in the positive and negative directions. In the positive direction the values get larger and larger. In the negative direction the values get closer and closer to -3.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L10-ACTIVITY-038",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Using Knowledge of Zeros",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The power to which a factor occurs in the factored form of a polynomial. For example, in the polynomial \\((x-1)^2(x+3)\\), the factor \\(x-1\\) has multiplicity 2 and the factor \\(x+3\\) has multiplicity 1.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
